{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Do not fear him</b> — For Moses was afraid lest the merit of Abraham stand in Og's favor, as it is stated concerning him: \"And the fugitive came and told Abram the Hebrew\" (Genesis 14:13), and this is Og, who escaped from the Refa'im whom Amraphel and his companions struck at Ashterot Karnayim. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 21:34) from the words of our Rabbis (Numbers Rabbah 19:24). Some have raised objections to this as well, in their opinion, that Moses our Teacher should not fear an arm of flesh, for HaShem our Elohim is with him, and all the nations are as nothing before Him — they are reckoned to Him as nothingness and void. And he is the one who exhorts Israel: \"Do not fear and do not be dismayed because of them\" (Deuteronomy 31:6), and he is the one who rebukes them for their fear, as it says concerning the spies: \"And I said to you, do not be dismayed and do not fear them, HaShem your Elohim who goes before you, He shall fight for you\" (Deuteronomy 1:29–30). Rather, Moses was afraid on account of the merit that he knew Og possessed.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense of the text, it had not been in Moses' mind to dispossess for Israel at this time the land of Sihon and Og, as I have explained (above, verses 21–22). And behold, Sihon had come out to meet Israel in the wilderness and fought against them by force; then the HaShem informed him: \"See, I have begun to deliver Sihon and his land before you — begin, take possession\" (Deuteronomy 2:31), for from here began the conquest of the seven nations. But Og gathered all his army at Edrei, which is a city at the edge of his border, and Israel could have turned aside from him just as they turned aside from Esau. And HaShem said to him: Do not fear him and go to him and engage him in battle, for I have given him into your hand. And in Midbar Sinai Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 19:32) I saw: Why was he afraid? He said: perhaps Israel transgressed in the war against Sihon, or became entangled in sin. And all of this is aggadah, and such is the fear of all the righteous, and so too with Joshua (Joshua 8:1)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The citations from Deuteronomy 1:29–30 are given by Ramban as a single combined reference '(שם א כט ל)', pointing to Deuteronomy 1:29–30; rendered with an en-dash."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:42.238983630Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}